    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d700">

                <pb facs="0597" n="585" xml:id="pg585"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890F.6363/44: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Saudi Arabia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Moose</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Jidda</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-09-14T14:00:00+03:06">September 14,
                            1942—2 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received September 15—12:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>64. Latter part Department’s 35, August 31 [<hi rendition="#italic">28</hi>], 10
                    p.m. British authorities in Cairo asked for comment on possible request to Saudi
                    Government to permit installation of anti-aircraft batteries and maintenance of
                    about 100 American troops at Dhahran to protect oil properties. After
                    consultation with King’s advisers, probably meaning Yusuf Yassin,<anchor n="43"
                        xml:id="d700-f1"/> the British Minister is giving his opinion substantially
                    as follows: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>That the King will agree to installation of AA defenses and presence
                            of American troops.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>That King’s attitude will be that he is defending Saudi territory with
                            American aid and although generous in admitting United States Army
                            personnel he might stipulate that they should leave after they have
                            properly trained a sufficient number of Saudi troops. The training
                            period might take a long time.</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>That British Minister understood that there is a plan to train certain
                            Casoc employees for defense duties and that the number of necessary
                            military personnel would be reduced thereby.</item>
                        <label>4.</label>
                        <item>That in training Saudi troops for AA defenses, it might be necessary
                            to employ Egyptian-Arabic-speaking AA specialists as
                            interpreters.</item>
                        <label>5.</label>
                        <item>That this Legation should approach the King in the matter while
                            British Minister would communicate British approval of plan.</item>
                    </list>
                </p>
                <p>British Minister and Yusuf Yassin are spending the current fast of Ramadhan in
                    Taif and the foregoing was obtained from British Legation First Secretary. I
                    know nothing of plan mentioned in paragraph 3 above and cannot readily confirm
                    it due to absence of Casoc representative from Jidda. However, it does not
                    affect the essence of the problem.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Cairo.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moose</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
